[Music]  [Applause]  China's one-child policy is regarded as  the biggest social experiment in the  human history I'm honored to be a  subject in this experiment I am one of  the 115 million only children in my  generation in 2015 when I first came to  the United States for Business School  China jobs at the one-child policy after  35 years many GSB classmates showed a  greater empathy to me saying Iran I'm so  happy for you that China eventually  dropped this evil policy how could the  Chinese government did not human rights  like that it must be really hard for you  growing up without siblings I cannot  imagine that people could not make their  own choices about how many kids to have  I really appreciated there is compassion  but deep inside I feel conflicted then  some other classmates further asked how  do you view the one-child policy  was it good or bad my response I don't  know since then I have tried to learn  more in hopes of arriving a better  answer today let me start with my own  story as the only child try to imagine  that you are a 5 year old only child  living in China like I was I have to  tell you that you have to spend some  time alone talking to yourself playing  by yourself  sometimes even hugging yourself because  you have to fly through rows in the same  time  princes and Prince but trust me  life was not that bad I had a great time  I had great companionship with my  cousins and soup and neighborhood kids  and I probably have to admit that I  cannot understand the whole concept of  siblings because all my peers are only  children as well so the ideal having  sibling never crossed my mind at home at  school we were told that the one-child  policy was fundamentally important  China to keep the population a great  size and to ensure the quality of life  at home my mom who has three other  siblings tell me a story about back in  1960s four of them had to split one loaf  of bread for a meal and all her clothes  are passed down from her to elderly  sisters  I also heard story from people of my own  age in Europe part of China where the  one-child policy had not been enforced  girls had to give away opportunities for  education for the boys at home so with  all these stories in mind I truly  believe that the one-child policy was  the right way to go for me for my family  as well as for my country until one day  in 2013 I was still working China as a  sales manager riding a bullet train from  Beijing to Shanghai I got a long message  from my mom which was very unusual she  wrote that she had been diagnosed with  the heart disease called HDD and she  needed open chest surgery as soon as  possible  she has hesitated to tell me because my  working schedules were full but she  eventually decided to let me know  because the risk of their surgery is  super high so at that moment actually I  was on a business trip with several  colleagues and also my my clients  sitting right in front of me but I burst  into tears I was terrified by the  thought what could happen to my mom I  also feel troubled how could my mom  hesitate to tell me I was her only child  I should be the person to keep company  with her take off care of her and sign  on the conscience form for the surgery  but at that moment she was still  thinking about disturbing my work this  is ridiculous so I look outside of the  train window  feel terrified helpless and as was  obligated and for the very first  the time I wished I could have a sibling  to share this fear and overcome the  hardship together so a few months later  I get back to my hometown I was very  happy to spend more time with my mom and  family members but in the meantime I was  local struggled with handling both her  work and both of her Jonas and my work  so but I'm very lucky because the  surgery was very successful and my mom  recovered however I kept thinking about  this idea of having a sibling how could  my life be different if I was not the  only child recently I send this question  to over 100 only Cal friends in China  here are some responses are really  interesting I will share with you if I  were not the only child I wouldn't be  forced to go on so many blind dates set  up by my parents however my parents  wouldn't be able to pay for the down  payments for a new apartment for me  which is as regarded as a must to get  married in China  here is one struck me most I might not  be as successful as I am because I  couldn't monopolize all the attention  and resources from my parents but I  would be more free leaving abroad  marrying to my same-sex partner instead  of living the life to meet my parents  expectations my friends are as  conflicted as I am the good is  inseparable from the bad scholars have  examined the one-child policy from many  different perspectives criticizing the  policy for violating human rights or  creating big social issues such as  little Emperor syndrome  aka China spoiler breath as a little in  program myself I am very grateful for  all the privileges I've received as an  only child  especially for the improved  opportunities for women  however I have to admit that the  excessive love and attention also  creates larger pressure after the  termination of the one-child policy I  had a conversation with my mom how many  kids do you think I should have I asked  she urged me to have a Lisa to in light  of all the downside of being the only  child but I'm not sure since I can  imagine that my future husband and I  have to support for elderly parents  adding two or more kids that's crazy and  especially given the social expectation  for taking care of the parents is way  much higher in Chinese culture and what  seems me more is the thought that if  something bad falls on me what could  happen to my mom I cannot imagine what  my mom would go through if she lost her  only child my same father is a  sociologist  specializing in the study of families  who lost their only child she told me  that now there are more than 1 million  such families in China these parents  generally are too old to give another  birth to the baby so they lost their  only hope many of suffer from tremendous  emotional trauma and recover all  hardships in terms of finance and also  life so I think this helps me to explain  part of my survey why my generation  carries a heavier by emotional burden  and is less willing to take risks with  the one-child policy good or bad to be  honest I still don't have the answer but  here is the lesson I want to share with  you were you from the outside it seems  easy to evaluate a government policy or  a business decision that had impact on  other people once you move to the inside  it's not just like snap your finger and  get to the answer to that question so I  would urge you and myself think about  the big choices and decisions on an  individual level healthy their life  stories and when you have the power to  make a decision for large group of  people no matter your political leader  or business leader try to take out the  view from the inside do not let the view  the outside dominant your choice and  this is my lesson from the one-child  policy thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

